{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99cf1dc9c01d76fe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9002: Integrated GenAI model evaluation for RHOAI\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. The strategy spans five\n13\tplatform components (TrustyAI, Llama Stack, Dashboard, Data Science Pipelines,\n14\tModel Registry), all of which exist in the platform architecture context, so no\n15\tonboarding chains are needed. All five are Konflux-onboarded already.\n16\t\n17\tTriage: NOT below-threshold (five components, five owning teams, multiple\n18\tarchitecturally distinct work streams: evaluation execution backend, Dashboard\n19\tUI, pipeline integration, Llama Stack provider, documentation). NOT docs-only.\n20\tFull decomposition follows.\n21\t\n22\t## Epic List\n23\t\n24\tMain plan:\n25\t\n26\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n27\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E001 | Investigation: Evaluation execution engine architecture | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E002 | Investigation: Canonical evaluation result schema | Investigation | Model Registry | Model Registry | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E003 | Investigation: Benchmark dataset redistribution licensing | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E004 | Standard benchmark suite catalog and packaging | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E005 | Dashboard evaluation run views and model version comparison | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E006 | Evaluation step for Data Science Pipelines | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | P1 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E007 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform evaluation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E008 | End-to-end evaluation workflow documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P0 |\n36\t\n37\tConditional branch epics (plan documents attached to the execution engine\n38\tinvestigation, not standalone Jira issues):\n39\t\n40\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n41\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E009 | Extend LMEvalJob as the single evaluation execution path | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E009 | New evaluation service for execution and the eval provider API | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n44\t\n45\tNotes on the epic list:\n46\t\n47\t- The documentation epic is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n48\t  (strategy frontmatter priority Critical -> P0), even though HLR-9 is P2.\n49\t- Component mappings are verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. The\n50\t  strategy's Affected Components section lists bare component names with no\n51\t  change descriptions or owners; owning teams were taken from the architecture\n52\t  context files (TrustyAI -> TrustyAI, Llama Stack -> RHOAI GenAI, Dashboard ->\n53\t  RHOAI Dashboard, Data Science Pipelines -> RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry ->\n54\t  Model Registry).\n55\t- Model Registry is treated as a passive component for code purposes: its\n56\t  custom properties are the existing, documented extension point for attaching\n57\t  evaluation results, and the writer is the evaluation execution path. The\n58\t  Model Registry engagement is the result-schema investigation plus validation\n59\t  acceptance criteria on the execution epics.\n60\t\n61\t## Dependency DAG\n62\t\n63\t```mermaid\n64\tgraph TD\n65\t    E001[\"E001 Execution engine investigation\"]\n66\t    E002[\"E002 Result schema investigation\"]\n67\t    E003[\"E003 Benchmark licensing investigation\"]\n68\t    E004[\"E004 Standard suite catalog\"]\n69\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard eval views\"]\n70\t    E006[\"E006 Pipelines eval step\"]\n71\t    E007[\"E007 Llama Stack eval provider\"]\n72\t    E008[\"E008 Evaluation documentation\"]\n73\t    BE009A[\"BRANCH-A-E009 Extend LMEvalJob (conditional)\"]\n74\t    BE009B[\"BRANCH-B-E009 New evaluation service (conditional)\"]\n75\t\n76\t    E001 --> E005\n77\t    E001 --> E006\n78\t    E001 --> E007\n79\t    E001 --> E008\n80\t    E002 --> E005\n81\t    E003 --> E004\n82\t    E004 --> E008\n83\t    E005 --> E008\n84\t    E006 --> E008\n85\t    E007 --> E008\n86\t    E001 -.-> BE009A\n87\t    E002 -.-> BE009A\n88\t    E001 -.-> BE009B\n89\t    E002 -.-> BE009B\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tSolid arrows are main-plan edges; dashed arrows gate conditional branch epics\n93\t(branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path).\n94\t\n95\t## DAG Justification\n96\t\n97\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n98\t|------|------|-----------|\n99\t| E001 → E005, E006, E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The execution engine decision (build on LMEvalJob vs new evaluation service) determines which API/CR surface the Dashboard reads, the pipeline step calls, and the Llama Stack provider backs onto. True gates with `rewrite` impact — the downstream epics exist either way but their approach changes. |\n100\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 | Rule 3 + conditional decomposition | The investigation has two bounded outcomes; the execution-path implementation epic is selected conditionally (see Conditional Branches). |\n101\t| E002 → E005, BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The canonical result schema determines the fields the Dashboard renders and the record the execution path persists. True gate, `rewrite` impact. |\n102\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The licensing classification determines whether each standard suite is bundled or customer-fetched — the packaging approach of the suite catalog. True gate, `rewrite` impact. |\n103\t| E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n104\t| E001 → E008 | Conditional-decomposition rule | The documentation epic must also wait for the execution-path decision; because main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics, it blocks on the gating Investigation instead. |\n105\t| No edge E004 ↔ E005/E006/E007 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | Suite catalog (TrustyAI repo), Dashboard, Pipelines, and Llama Stack live in different repos and share no build-against artifacts. |\n106\t| No edge E005 ↔ E006 ↔ E007 | Rule 14 | Different components/teams/repos; each integrates independently with the (investigation-selected) execution surface. |\n107\t| No edge between E001, E002, E003 | Rule: independent unknowns | The three investigations resolve different unknowns with different deliverables and can run in parallel. |\n108\t| Branch epics not in any main-plan `dependencies` | Branch rule 2 | Branch epics are plan documents, not main-plan nodes; downstream dependencies on the outcome attach to the Investigation. |\n109\t\n110\t## Conditional Branches\n111\t\n112\tThe execution engine investigation (E001) has two bounded outcomes, stated by\n113\tthe strategy's first open question:\n114\t\n115\t- **Branch A — build on LMEvalJob**: TrustyAI's LMEvalJob (Tech Preview)\n116\t  becomes the single execution path; it is extended to cover launching\n117\t  evaluations against served models, custom evaluation tasks, and result\n118\t  persistence. `RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E009`.\n119\t- **Branch B — new evaluation service**: a new evaluation service is built\n120\t  (under the TrustyAI component, the platform's model-quality component) that\n121\t  executes evaluations and also serves Llama Stack's eval provider API.\n122\t  `RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E009`.\n123\t\n124\tBoth branch epics carry HLR-1, HLR-3, HLR-4, and HLR-8 (execution, persistence,\n125\tcustom tasks, cost/duration metadata — one codebase, one result record, one\n126\tlogical change; see HLR bundling notes below). Each branch epic is gated by the\n127\tinvestigation (`gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`, also in its `dependencies`)\n128\twith `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. Main-plan epics that depend on the\n129\toutcome (Dashboard, Pipelines step, Llama Stack provider, docs) depend on the\n130\tInvestigation epic, not on a branch outcome.\n131\t\n132\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n133\t\n134\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n135\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n136\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Launch evaluation of a served model against a benchmark suite | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional) |\n137\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Standard benchmark suites out of the box | E004 |\n138\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Results persisted and linked to Model Registry version | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional) |\n139\t| HLR-4 | P1 | Custom evaluation tasks through the same flow | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional, bundled with HLR-1) |\n140\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard shows runs, status, scores, side-by-side comparison | E005 |\n141\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Evaluation as a Data Science Pipeline step | E006 |\n142\t| HLR-7 | P1 | Llama Stack eval provider API backed by platform evaluation | E007 |\n143\t| HLR-8 | P2 | Cost/duration metadata on evaluation runs | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional, bundled with HLR-3) |\n144\t| HLR-9 | P2 | End-to-end product documentation incl. custom task authoring | E008 |\n145\t\n146\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n147\t\n148\tHLR bundling decisions:\n149\t\n150\t- **HLR-1 + HLR-4** stay in one epic: the strategy states custom tasks run\n151\t  \"through the same flow\" — same execution surface, same API, not independently\n152\t  shippable. Not a deferral signal.\n153\t- **HLR-3 + HLR-8** stay in one epic: cost/duration metadata (tokens,\n154\t  wall-clock, GPU time) is recorded in the same result record that is persisted\n155\t  to Model Registry custom properties — the same data structure and write path.\n156\t  Splitting would build the same persistence surface twice.\n157\t- **HLR-5** is not split from comparison: run/status/score views and the\n158\t  side-by-side comparison are the same Dashboard surface over the same result\n159\t  data.\n160\t\n161\tPriority inheritance: E001, E002, E003 are P0 because they gate P0 work\n162\t(HLR-1/2/3). E004 is P0 (HLR-2). The branch execution epics are P0 (HLR-1/3,\n163\tand HLR-4/8 are bundled on the same surface). E005/E006/E007 are P1 (they\n164\tenable only P1 HLRs; the P0 docs epic does not push priority upstream per the\n165\tdocs-authoring exception). E008 is P0 via the docs-authoring exception\n166\t(strategy priority Critical → P0).\n167\t\n168\t## Health Warnings\n169\t\n170\tNon-blocking — the decomposition proceeds; humans should verify:\n171\t\n172\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n173\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no\n174\t   Non-Functional Requirements, and no Technical Approach section. Team\n175\t   assignments were derived from architecture context owning teams; epic\n176\t   acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Any NFRs the authors\n177\t   intended are not represented.\n178\t2. **P0 capability on a Tech Preview foundation.** If Branch A is selected, a\n179\t   P0 evaluation capability builds on the LMEvalJob lm-eval integration, which\n180\t   architecture context marks Tech Preview. Reliability risk; the branch A epic\n181\t   carries an acceptance criterion that existing LMEvalJob behavior remains\n182\t   functional.\n183\t3. **Docs epic priority inversion (apparent).** HLR-9 is P2 but the\n184\t   docs-authoring epic is P0 because the docs-authoring exception derives\n185\t   priority from the strategy's Critical priority. Flagged for reviewer\n186\t   awareness; the rule governs.\n187\t4. **Docs epic cannot directly block on branch epics.** Main-plan epics must\n188\t   not depend on branch epics, so the documentation epic blocks on the\n189\t   execution engine Investigation as the proxy. Reviewers should confirm docs\n190\t   work actually starts after the selected branch's execution implementation\n191\t   lands.\n192\t5. **Open question 2 requires a prototype before commitment.** The result\n193\t   schema investigation must run against real MMLU output before the\n194\t   persistence implementation is committed (strategy wording: \"prototype\n195\t   against real MMLU output before committing\").\n196\t\n197\t## Tiered Delivery\n198\t\n199\tNot applicable. The strategy has no external dependency with uncertain timing\n200\tthat gates user-facing value: all work depends on platform-internal decisions\n201\t(the three investigations), and lm-evaluation-harness is already integrated via\n202\tLMEvalJob. There is no upstream PR/RFC with uncontrolled acceptance timing in\n203\tthe critical path, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is warranted.\n204\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95d5379a6620c5ce","agent_id":"a36f2c95917215dda","session_id":"544b3ec5-06d3-46ca-8ea6-a4c49a0f57f5","uuid":"f40be018-5a51-41eb-bfa0-0792aed5f3a1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:20:24.038Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-99cf1dc9c01d76fe","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
